---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dofCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · A robot state is a point', note: 'Every possible position of the robot — all joint angles at once — is one point in an abstract space: the configuration space, or C-space.' },
    { math: '2 · Count the degrees of freedom', note: 'Grübler\'s formula for a mechanism: DOF = (sum of joint freedoms) − (constraints imposed by loops). A planar arm with three revolute joints has DOF = 3.' },
    { math: '3 · The C-space has obstacles too', note: 'A joint angle that drives the gripper into a wall is forbidden. Obstacles in the room become obstacle regions in C-space.' },
    { math: '4 · Planning = finding a path', note: 'Motion planning is now a geometry problem: connect the start point to the goal point without entering any forbidden region.' },
    { math: '5 · Dimension is the enemy', note: 'A 6-DOF arm has a 6-dimensional C-space; a humanoid has 30+. Grids die of dimensionality — which is why sampling-based planners exist.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/robotics/foundational/configuration-space">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Robotics' first abstraction is also its most powerful: replace the robot with a point. The
    <strong>configuration space</strong> (C-space) assigns one coordinate per degree of freedom, so
    the whole robot — however many arms and joints — becomes a single point in a high-dimensional
    space. Planning motion becomes path-finding in that space, and the entire field's algorithms
    are organized around this move.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from robot to point: the C-space move" widget="equationStep" config={dofCfg} />

  <h2>Counting degrees of freedom</h2>
  <p>
    Grübler's formula makes DOF arithmetic honest. A planar four-bar linkage: four links, four
    revolute joints, one loop — DOF = 3(4−1) − 2·4 = 1, so the whole mechanism has exactly one
    motion. A free body in the plane has 3 DOF; in space, 6. Get the count wrong and the mechanism
    jams or flops, no matter how good the motors are.
  </p>

  <h2>Obstacles move into the space too</h2>
  <p>
    The C-space's cleverest trick is transforming collision: instead of checking whether a
    complicated robot hits a wall, compute the set of configurations where it would — a "forbidden
    region" — and forbid the point from entering it. Planning around furniture becomes navigating a
    map. The geometry is harder to compute, but the problem becomes uniform.
  </p>

  <h2>Why sampling won</h2>
  <p>
    A grid over a 6-DOF space needs millions of cells per axis-pair; exhaustive search dies. Modern
    planners — RRT, PRM — throw random samples at the space, wire nearby ones together, and grow a
    road map or a tree toward the goal. They trade completeness for high probability of success,
    which is the right trade once the dimension passes three. Everything after this course —
    kinematics, dynamics, control — lives inside this space.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lynch &amp; Park, <em>Modern Robotics</em>, Ch. 2 (official companion site); LaValle, <em>Planning Algorithms</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
